  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Expressed as an **improper fraction** in its simplest form, $\\frac{2}{3}÷\\frac{4}{9}=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "Dividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction.\n\nThe reciprocal of $\\frac{4}{9}$ is $\\frac{9}{4}.$ So we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}÷\\frac{4}{9}=\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we multiply fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{2\\times 9}{3\\times 4}=\\frac{18}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{18}{12}=\\frac{18÷6}{12÷6}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$",
